  "content": {
    "id": "p32-challenge-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "$x^5+2x^3+ax^2+b$ is divisible by $x^3+1$. Find the values of $a$ and $b$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=1$ and $b=2$.",
      "worked": "Let $P(x)=x^5+2x^3+ax^2+b$. Dividing by $x^3+1$, the first quotient term is $x^2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}P(x)-x^2(x^3+1)&=2x^3+(a-1)x^2+b.\\end{align*}$$\nThe next quotient term is $2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}P(x)-(x^2+2)(x^3+1)&=(a-1)x^2+b-2.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThis remainder has degree less than $3$. Divisibility requires it to be the zero polynomial, so\n$$\\begin{align*}a-1&=0\\\\a&=1\\\\b-2&=0\\\\b&=2.\\end{align*}$$\nCheck:\n$$x^5+2x^3+x^2+2=(x^3+1)(x^2+2).$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 60
  },
